# Blackbird, verse (figure 139.1)
# The guitar's two outer voices in parallel tenths, one system per phrase; the open G string that
# drones between them on the record is not in the chart and is left out.
# Octaves follow the guitar: bass on the low strings (G2 A2 B2 ...), top voice a tenth above.
# Phrase A: Pollack marks the first bar "half" (the phrase is "two beats longer than three
# measures"), so its two chords are written as a two-beat pickup. His lower-case "b" over "g" in
# bar 3 is read as the B-over-G tenth an octave lower: the line climbs G/B, A/C, B/D and drops back
# to G/B.
# "X -" is a note held for the whole bar. In the last phrase Pollack leaves the bass blank under
# the C-natural (V7) and types only a hold; read as the A carrying over from the bar before.
# No chord symbols are typed, so the roman numerals go under the bass.
#? Bar 3 of phrase A: lower-case b/g read as an octave drop (B3 over G2); check against the record.
#? Last phrase, V7 bar: the bass is blank in the typed chart; held A2 is a guess (a D might be meant by "V7").
